2026-10-10

Rooms, portals and collision (the "impossible geometry" work)
* [perf] The plan reaches a room by every path of portals to it, and draws it whole each time (the stencil and the portal's plane throw most of it away on the GPU). From rm08 room 203 it is 83 nodes, rooms 520 and 501 drawn 12 times each, 28200 triangles where the whole level is 9677; rm17 room 24 is 16335 against 5884. Both are faster with --no-cull (1.5 against 3.5 ms a whole frame on rm08, 1.2 against 2.2 on rm17).
* [perf] The world is drawn in immediate mode, a glBegin for each material group and layer: about 0.5 us each on this machine, whatever is in it. Display lists or vertex buffers per room would leave the per-room clip planes as they are.
* [perf] Standing in a doorway costs more: in rm05's cabin door (--pos 37391.7,32932.0,-36566.3 --yaw -155.1 --pitch -5.8) the render takes a little over 1 ms, and a step either way (deeper into the cabin, or out toward the stairs) brings it down. Likely the doorway rule: within the opening the player stands in both rooms, both are drawn whole and cut, and the plan starts from each. rm04 room 602's adjacent doorway has the same oddity.
* [perf] About 0.7 ms a frame outside rendering is still unaccounted for. Suspects: floor_room and the ray queries scan every triangle instead of using the spatial grid; also the per-frame room logic, NPC updates, HUD. Profile first.
* [collision] No hysteresis on the floor-owner room. Where two rooms' floors meet it can flip every frame, changing what is drawn and rebuilding the collision set (O(triangles x listed rooms)).
* [collision] The player's walls follow the game's collision groups (collision.rs, from each object's `tags` and `ind`, decoded from RogueSpear.exe 0x55eeb0). `tags` lists an object's collision faces, each a drawn face (group, face in group) or, where its group is 0xffff, a triangle of the `collisions` vertices on one of its planes; `ind` groups them under a name and flags. A group with flag 2 is no walls: trees, grass, parts of much furniture, and exterior shells (rm11's room 1, rm02's 605), while undrawn faces in other groups stand in (trunk boxes, rm17's freight cars). Flags seen: 1 on ladder rungs, 2 no walls, 4 on undrawn faces, 8 bullets and grenades pass (below), 0x10 floors, 0x20 on exterior copies, 0x40 a floor however steep, 0x80 on "static..." groups (rm02's captain's chairs, rm03's couches). Not done:
  - Floors still come from every upward face, flag 2 or not (rm11's courtyard ground is flagged 0x22, and it must hold the player). The original's loader takes floors with a normal's y of at least 0.775 (pi/4 - 0.01), walls under 0.795 and drops faces further down; where a room has a 0x10 group, only 0x10 and 0x40 groups give floors. Ours: 0.643 (50 degrees) for both. Where its player's floors come from is not traced (0x54c490 casts down through 0x561950 and accepts a steep floor only with flag 0x40).
  - Groups named "static..." are built apart by the loader (0x55fb60, 0x55f640); ours treats them as plain faces.
  - Furniture is often flagged 2 only in part: rm05's passenger seats keep their armpieces and side panels in 0 groups and have undrawn boxes (0x4), so they still block, as in the original (checked 2026-10-22). Check against the rough scan in examples/lost-walls.rs (rm03's pipes and trusses, rm04's room 960 walls), which lists drawn walls in groups flagged 2 with no undrawn wall near them; it overstates (rm17's freight cars block, through their undrawn boxes).
  - The collisions toggle (--collisions) still shows only the `collisions` vertices as points.
* [render] data/texture/Rommel.CXP (src/cxp.rs) is read for color keys, and for which textures bullets and grenades pass. Its animated textures (rm11's fireplace and bubbles cycle every 0.1 s or less), scrolling ones (no map uses any) and mipmap settings are parsed but not used. The one texture of type 1, "oscillate", is rm09's security monitor (securitymon01..03 every 0.1 s, on the console in room 211); read as 1-2-3-2-1, unconfirmed: in the original it is fast flickering static either way. Those monitors are copies of a dynamic object (211_dyn_monitor_static) that we do not place in the level, as we do only doors and glass, so they are not drawn at all.
* [collision] Bullets and keyed textures. Bullets go through Rommel.CXP's `gunpass` textures (rm06's railing cha_rail_01, rm13's chain fence) and grenades through its `grenadepass` ones (the railing, not the fence). That fits the original on rm06: the panels and posts are one gunpass texture, and the top rail that stops shots is trim_rustic_02 over it. Checked in game (2026-10-21): grenades go through rm06's railing, and rm13's fence stops grenades but not bullets.
  - Blasts (grenade::clear_between, through CollisionWorld::blast_hit) go through the faces bullets pass and through swinging doors, and walls and sliding doors stop them. Checked in the original (2026-10-21): a blast beyond rm13's fence hurts, one beyond a wall does not; wood doors and rm09's single metal doors (12_doorextshrt05, 12_doorint_short01, 210_doorexterior01, 210_doorinterior01) let it through and are blown apart, though no door texture outside rm09's chain-link gates is gunpass. rm09's sliding doors (204_doorsliding_*) are not broken; that a closed one stops the blast is inferred from that, not seen, as the grenade opens them through their touchplate first (half open, the blast got through). Not done: destroying the door. Unchecked: a keyed texture without gunpass (rm05's netting, rm18's iron gate). The surface a blast scorches is still found through every face.
  - Bots see players through what bullets go through. The user expects the original's terrorists to see through rm13's fence, not confirmed.
  - Bullets, grenades and blasts go through faces in collision groups flagged 8, whatever the texture (collision.rs SHOT_THROUGH). That is how rm05's cargo netting (05_cargonetting_48_24_24.bmp: colorkey, no gunpass) lets shots through room 202's netting wall (x 37574; --pos 37657.0,32482.0,-32649.8 --yaw -318.9 --pitch -8.2) and stops them at room 316's netting cage (x 37957; --pos 38013.6,32932.0,-31328.8 --yaw 150.9 --pitch -3.3), and how grenades go through both the netting wall and rm15's camouflage net (15_net_camo.tga, gunpass only) (both checked in the original 2026-10-22). The net is in room 504 flagged 8 and again in the exterior, room 507, unflagged and partly triangulated another way; standing under it the exterior is solid too, so a face does not stop a shot where another room's face of the same material, crossed within a unit, lets it through (CollisionWorld::nearest). The original likely meets only the room the shot or grenade is in; doing that instead of the rooms around the player would make the copy rule unnecessary. Unchecked in the original: the other groups flagged 8 (wooden boards and decks in rm13's rooms 500 and 93 and rm15's rooms 50 and 51, 06_w_shed_wall5.bmp; rm12's globe lights; ladder posts; rm07's studio catwalks).
  - rm12's catwalk railing (rail_metal_opera_01.bmp, drawn from TGArail_metal_opera_01.rsb, its only texture) stops grenades in the original (checked 2026-10-22), as ours does: the .bmp has no CXP entry (rail_metal_opera_01.tga has alphablend and gunpass) and its collision groups are 0 and 2. Ours stops bullets there too; unchecked in the original, which may look up the .tga's properties for it.
  - Player collision with railings and fences has not been compared yet.
* [test] nested_in, level_at, player_rooms and the clipped collision of a touched doorway still have no tests (solid_rooms has one, for a neighbor beyond a portal). Copy the data-conditional tests in visibility.rs (skip when the map is missing), with rm11 for the hall/stairwell cases.
* [collision] Floors (ground_at) ignore the room filter, since a big hall is often several rooms with no portal between them, and a floor under the feet never traps anyone. Walls and bullets are still filtered by room. floor-walk shows a handful of stacked-floor steps on rm11 (floors 45+ apart in the same place) that are artifacts of how it looks, not holes.
* [levels] Visibility::new decides which room is the outdoors from the whole map's extent, and door leaves and glass panes (stored in their own space near the origin) stretch that extent: on rm11 it runs from x = -140, so the courtyard shell (room 1) is not flagged. Use the rooms' floor plans instead, after checking what that changes for walls inside the buildings.
* [render] Standing in a doorway, each room is cut where it overlaps the other, but only within a few units (WALL) of the other room's edge, so a hairline of 121's stairs shows from the 119 doorway on rm11. A bigger inset would remove it and bring back the courtyard wall gap; telling stray surfaces from real ones needs more than geometry.
* [render] Bullet-hole decals, glass panes and the viewmodel ignore rooms. A decal on the wall of a room that is not drawn can show in the void. Characters show only when a room they stand in is drawn, but in the default drawing they still show through walls where a portal's opening had its depth wiped and the room beyond has nothing there (rm08, by the canal).
* [levels] The thresholds were tuned on rm05 and rm11 only: TOUCH 40, NESTED 0.6, margin 20% (max 100), level slack 60, nearest room 150, outdoors at 50% of the map, polygon offset 2, plan budget 96 and depth 8. Run floor-scan and F2 dumps on the other maps (rm01..., citystreet) before trusting them.
* [render] When the plan hits its budget (96 portals) or depth limit (8), rooms silently do not draw. Log it, or show it in the F2 dump.
* [render] Without a texture a face is not drawn at all, in textured and wireframe mode alike, so with only the committed bare-bones data `cargo demo` shows almost nothing. Fall back to flat color (textured) or plain lines (wireframe).
* [render] A character seen through another player's visor (the .tga part of a character model, blended in draw_character's second pass) does not show: the visor hides it. This predates the vertex-array change (checked against the commit before it). Unchecked suspicion: the blended visor still writes depth, so whatever is drawn after it behind the glass fails the depth test; draw order of players against each other would matter too. Try glDepthMask(0) around the has_alpha pass, as with the level's soft sprites (SPRITE_MODES in main.rs). Reproduce with two characters, one behind the other's visor (first seen in an F2 dump screenshot).

Game
* [doors] OneTimeTouchplate gates are not handled: vehicle gates and sealed doors on rm09 and rm15, sliding gates on rm09 and rm19 (they open when something stands on a plate). In the original a grenade landing on rm09's sliding doors' plate opens them too.
* [doors] The .qob attachments listed on each door (woodwarmdklng1.qob and the like) are not drawn. The leaf looks complete without them; check against the original.
* [doors] Swing direction is the sign of the angle in the map, about the hinge (the local axis that points up). It matches the original where checked: rm06's 04_door pairs and rm05's 20_doormetal doors. The rest, including the other tipped doors (rm05 13_doormetal02, rm08, rm12, rm17), are not recorded as checked (some doors swing toward the player).
* [doors] A pair of doors can open together in the original, which we do not do. In rm06 and rm10 the four 04_door leaves (03/04 at x 33902, 01/02 at x 35432) open as a pair, swinging apart on open and in on close, while rm06's 15_doorstandard pair opens one leaf at a time, as ours do. Nothing in the record tells them apart (checked on every map): every field of the two pairs matches, and tipped doors (X up, 25 of them) are mostly single leaves, so the tipped transform is not the signal. Likely in the engine, maybe keyed on the name (NN_doorNN); untested.
* [doors] A door whose partner shares its portal matches no portal: door::doors needs the leaf's middle within PORTAL_MATCH (40) of the portal's middle, and one portal spans both leaves of rm06's 04_door pairs (190 wide, the leaf middle 47 away) and its 15_doorstandard pair. Those doors have no portal; check what that does to the view through them, then match a portal by overlap with the leaf instead.
* [doors] Only the player opens doors, from a fixed reach of 220 units and 60 degrees. Unread fields: the touchplate's unknown1 is always 0, unknown2 varies by door model ([1, 25, 0], [15, 30, 0], [20, 35, 0], [25, 35, 0] and others; maybe the touchplate area), the shared unknown1 is 0, 2, 4 or 8 (0 on every tipped door; 4 on sliding doors in rm09, rm15 and rm19; not what makes a pair), and names holds only glass-pane names (rm15, rm19). No NPC use, breaching, locked doors or noise. A door can close on someone standing in the doorway.
* [doors] The doorway view while a door moves: the portal opens as soon as the door starts and shuts only once it has closed.
* [game] Enemy AI; it can use the same room and portal data for sight and hearing.
* [game] Q / E lean (the keys are free).
* [movement] Going forward or back with a strafe key, the original moves along the forward or back direction turned a fixed 27 degrees (pi * 0.15, the quaternions at 0x8224e8 / 0x8224d8 in RogueSpear.exe), the same angle the hips turn to. Ours adds the strafe velocity to the forward one and caps the length at the faster of the two (sim::move_player), so the path is off by atan(348 / forward speed): about 25 degrees running, 34 walking and 43 backing up, and crouched it differs again. Turn the forward or back velocity by movement::HIP_TURN instead; the speed is presumably the forward or back pace, but check in the exe. It changes gameplay (how far sideways a diagonal goes) and the sim, so the build check matters, and net::animation's SIDEWAYS (11 degrees) can then sit closer to 27.
* [game] Surface-specific footsteps; explosions that break glass (glass::Pane::distance_to is ready for it).

Housekeeping
* [build] src/map.old looks unused.
* [docs] docs/walking.md still mentions the deleted opencode.rs.
* [docs] README says the Linux build was not tried recently. Try it.
* [scripts] bench.sh drifts: two runs minutes apart differed by 30% on this machine while the same two builds run alternately matched within 3%. Compare against a baseline build, alternating the two.
* [sound] rodio prints "Dropping DeviceSink..." on exit.

Map parser
